# Cloudflare Pages reads this file to attach response headers to every
# request for the frontend. The API (Railway) already gets a solid default
# set from helmet() in server/src/app.js — this is the frontend's equivalent.
#
# The Content-Security-Policy below was built by walking every page's actual
# network/script/style/worker surface (grepping for external URLs, inline
# <script>/<style>, style="" attributes, Worker(), Audio(), CSS url()) rather
# than guessed, then verified against a local static server that replays this
# exact file's headers, driven by a real browser (Playwright) across all ten
# pages. Two things the walk would not have caught on its own, found only by
# that live pass:
#   - Clerk's SDK spins up its own internal worker from a `blob:` URL — plain
#     `worker-src 'self'` blocks it; needs `blob:` too.
#   - The sha256 a person hand-computes for an inline <script> tag is easy to
#     get subtly wrong (whitespace/boundary slicing). The hash below is the
#     one the browser itself reported as required, not a manual computation.
# script-src has no 'unsafe-inline': the one inline <script> in the codebase
# (pages/puzzles/index.html, the puzzle-prefetch) runs only via its exact-byte
# sha256 hash, so anything an attacker injected would not match and would not
# execute. style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' rather than trying to hash its way
# out of it — pages/database/script.js and pages/profile/script.js build
# style="" attributes with runtime-variable values (urls, percentages) via
# innerHTML, which no fixed hash can cover; locking that down for real would
# mean rewriting those call sites to use CSSOM/classes instead, which is a
# separate refactor, not a CSP change.
#
# Known gaps that only a signed-in production session can settle, both narrow
# in blast radius (neither can blank the app):
#   - img-src allows Clerk's own domain plus img.clerk.com for avatar images;
#     if a signed-in user's avatar actually resolves elsewhere, the failure
#     mode is a broken image icon, not a broken page.
#   - If this Clerk instance has bot-protection (Cloudflare Turnstile) turned
#     on for sign-up, its challenge iframe is not accounted for here and
#     would need frame-src/script-src/connect-src additions for
#     challenges.cloudflare.com — only sign-up would be affected, and it
#     would fail loudly (the widget never renders) rather than silently.
# api.usecheese.xyz is the only non-Clerk, non-cdnjs origin the *repo's own
# code* calls — confirmed by the grep sweep. It is not the only origin the
# *served page* calls: Cloudflare Pages itself injects a Cloudflare Web
# Analytics beacon (static.cloudflareinsights.com) into the HTML at the edge,
# after the file leaves this repo, so no grep of the source could ever have
# found it. That one was caught live, in production, as a real CSP violation
# — script-src blocked the beacon script, and it in turn posts back to
# cloudflareinsights.com, hence the connect-src entry alongside it. Anything
# else Cloudflare starts edge-injecting later will show the same way: a CSP
# violation naming the exact blocked origin, not a silent break.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://clerk.usecheese.xyz https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com 'sha256-apBXz3Yy/rYCQfDcy2gAU6hIPwRBIvmxO2yhLNJjtBk=' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://img.clerk.com https://clerk.usecheese.xyz; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://api.usecheese.xyz https://clerk.usecheese.xyz https://cloudflareinsights.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
